<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kōfu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kofu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kōfu is the capital of Yamanashi Prefecture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, in a basin west of Tokyo. It grew from 1519 around the Tsutsujigasaki residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-clan">Takeda clan</a>, rulers of Kai Province. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> it was a castle town, under direct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> rule for much of the time, and it is now a centre of fruit growing and wine production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kofu.jpg" alt="Kōfu" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Halowand · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kofu_montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kōfu lies in north-central Yamanashi Prefecture in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, in the Kōfu Basin, 250 to 300 metres above sea level and surrounded by mountains on all sides. Three quarters of its territory is part of Mount Kinpu in the north, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-fuji">Mount Fuji</a> can be seen in the distance to the south. Its name means capital of Kai Province. Archaeologists have found evidence of settlement from the Japanese Paleolithic period, continuing through the Jōmon, Yayoi and Kofun periods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period the provincial temple of Kai Province was established in what is now Kōfu, which suggests that the provincial capital stood nearby. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a> the Kai-Genji, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-clan">Minamoto clan</a>, ruled large estates there and developed a military force noted for its cavalry. By the Muromachi period the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-clan">Takeda clan</a>, a branch of the Kai-Genji, dominated the area and built a castle in what is now part of the city.</p><p>Under Takeda Nobutora Kōfu was rebuilt as a castle town from 1519, and it remained the capital of the Takeda under his successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> and Takeda Katsuyori. During the Edo period Kai Province was territory ruled directly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, and Kōfu Castle remained its administrative centre. In 1705 the domain was conferred on Yanagisawa Yoshiyasu, a favourite of the fifth shōgun, and in 1724 it passed to Yamato-Koriyama, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> held the castle directly again.</p><p>The town of Kōfu was proclaimed on 1 July 1889, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. A typhoon and the felling of forests brought a great flood in August 1907, in which 233 people died in Yamanashi Prefecture, the largest natural disaster of modern times there. On the night of 6 July 1945 United States B-29 bombers destroyed much of the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kōfu is the capital of Yamanashi Prefecture and a commercial and transport centre for its central part, with 187,985 inhabitants in 2019. Local industries include food processing, wine, textiles and crystalware. Historic sites include Kōfu Castle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsutsujigasaki-castle">Tsutsujigasaki Castle</a>, Takeda Shrine and the temple Kai Zenkō-ji. Each April the Shingen-ko Festival celebrates the memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> with parades.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kōfu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Takeda Nobutora unified the province of Kai after years of fighting local rivals and in 1519 founded a new seat at Tsutsujigasaki, around which the town of <b>Kofu</b> grew.</blockquote><a href="/state/takeda-clan-domain">Takeda clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
